posts.body:
Well, don't really take this as a solution, it's probably better to use some of those more robust plugins and stuff if you're trying to achieve something that is elegant. I made this more to see if I could than anything else. It's something that's just really simple and makes it so that 1) max HP can drop to 0 and 2) gauges don't crash if it's dropped to 0. There are probably bugs with it. You can't actually use a revival item when a character has 0 MHP this way so that's interesting.
I just did this as an exercise and I'm a JS noob so it's probably not your best bet xD but hey if it happens to do what someone wants it to I guess that's cool too, figured I'd post it anyway for the heck of it, took me like an hour to figure out lol.
Spoiler:
Code
JavaScript:
Game_BattlerBase.prototype.paramMin = function(paramId) {
    if (paramId === 0) {
        return 0;  // MHP
    } else if (paramId === 1) {
        return 0;    // MMP
    } else {
        return 1;
    }
};

Window_Base.prototype.drawGauge = function(x, y, width, rate, color1, color2) {
    rate = isNaN(rate) ? 0 : rate;
    var fillW = Math.floor(width * rate);
    var gaugeY = y + this.lineHeight() - 8;
    this.contents.fillRect(x, gaugeY, width, 6, this.gaugeBackColor());
    this.contents.gradientFillRect(x, gaugeY, fillW, 6, color1, color2);
};
You'd still want to run Troop events that check if an actor is at 0 HP, and if so if they are wearing the outfit, and then either remove 9999 from their Max HP or, if you plan on being able to bring them back, store their Max HP into a variable and remove that much instead. It'll be easier to restore whatever their Max HP used to be that way.
I still think it's probably better to remove them from the party and add some message saying they are gone forever or something along those lines, at least if you're trying to go with a no-plugin approach.
